cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite the preprint below. The Spanish version is under review at Texto Livre."
title: "Aligning Language Models with Indigenous Community Knowledge: Constitutional AI for Data Policies and Epistemic Justice from the Global South"
abstract: >-
  This work presents a complete system for aligning language models with the
  ethical and philosophical principles of indigenous communities in Latin
  America. Based on interviews with five community leaders and defenders who
  are members of URI (United Religions Initiative), we built an operational
  cultural constitution with 12 principles covering territory, coexistence,
  authority, well-being, technology, and language. On this basis, we
  implemented the Constitutional AI (CAI) pipeline in two phases: supervised
  fine-tuning (SFT) with a self-critique and constitution-guided revision
  loop, and direct preference optimization (DPO) with 24 pairs of aligned and
  misaligned responses. The base model used was Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct with LoRA
  adaptation. Three experimental configurations were run to estimate the
  robustness of results. The system was deployed in a conversational interface
  with retrieval-augmented generation (RAG).
